MEDBANK = [
    E('vancomycin 750 mg IV q24h (due 0900; last given 10/21 0900; 1,250 mg load given 10/20): glycopeptide, blocks cell-wall synthesis; empiric MRSA coverage until the final culture. HIGH-ALERT. Infuse over at least 90-120 min; hold and call pharmacy/provider for creatinine rise of 0.3 or more, urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/hr, or level above 20; ototoxicity; kidney risk with pip-tazo; order ends 10/23 0600.', 0, ''),
    E('piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn) 3.375 g IV q8h (0600, 1400, 2200), extended 4-hour infusion: penicillin plus beta-lactamase inhibitor; infected wound (E. coli, Enterococcus, anaerobes). Rash, diarrhea/C. difficile (loose stools, on antibiotics); order ends 10/24 0600; no penicillin allergy on chart.', 0, ''),
    E('lisinopril 20 mg daily: ACE inhibitor; hypertension/heart failure (home). Hold and notify for SBP below 100; check potassium and creatinine; extra kidney risk with sepsis, vancomycin, pip-tazo and furosemide; angioedema, cough.', 0, ''),
    E('carvedilol 6.25 mg BID: beta blocker; HFrEF. Give with food; hold and notify for HR below 55 or SBP below 100; do not stop abruptly; can blunt tachycardia (a sepsis sign) and hypoglycemia symptoms; donepezil adds bradycardia.', 0, ''),
    E('furosemide 40 mg daily: loop diuretic; heart failure (home). NO hold parameter on the order: ask; check BP, weight (none charted), lungs, edema, urine output, potassium 3.7, magnesium 1.7, creatinine; she is "euvolemic to mildly dry" and septic; wastes potassium and magnesium (refeeding).', 0, ''),
    E('insulin glargine (Lantus) 20 units at bedtime (2100): basal insulin; type 2 diabetes. Notify provider before giving if glucose below 100; intake is only 50-75%: hypoglycemia risk; "reduced 20% while NPO" wording does not match the chart.', 0, ''),
    E('insulin lispro (Humalog) low-dose correction scale ACHS (due 0730 and 1130): rapid-acting insulin; hyperglycemia. Check glucose first, give with a meal; glucose goal 140-180; scale numbers are not written in the order (clarify).', 0, ''),
    E('enoxaparin (Lovenox) 40 mg subcut daily (0600; given today): low-molecular-weight heparin; VTE prophylaxis (immobile). HIGH-ALERT. Hold and notify for active bleeding or platelets below 50; kidney clearance; Hgb 6.4 means ask the provider before tomorrow\'s dose.', 0, ''),
    E('acetaminophen 650 mg PO q6h (0600, 1200, 1800, 0000): non-opioid analgesic/antipyretic; multimodal pain control and premedication. 2.6 g/day: order reference says maximum 3 g/day, 2 g if malnourished; records temperature first (masks fever).', 0, ''),
    E('tramadol 25 mg PO q6h PRN pain 4-6: opioid analgesic (and serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects). HIGH-ALERT. Hold for RR below 10 or sedation; lowers seizure threshold, dizziness, nausea; chart has a codeine intolerance; Beers list caution (hyponatremia, falls, delirium).', 0, ''),
    E('hydromorphone (Dilaudid) 0.25 mg IV q3h PRN pain 7-10: opioid agonist. HIGH-ALERT. Hold for RR below 10, SpO2 below 90% or sedation; assess sedation, RR, SpO2 before and 15-30 minutes after; not yet given on the floor.', 0, ''),
    E('donepezil (Aricept) 10 mg at bedtime: cholinesterase inhibitor; dementia. Bradycardia (check HR), nausea, poor appetite/weight loss, vivid dreams; can worsen urge incontinence and QT prolongation (with ondansetron).', 0, ''),
    E('mirabegron (Myrbetriq) 25 mg ER daily: beta-3 agonist (relaxes bladder); overactive bladder. Swallow whole; hold and notify for SBP above 180; non-anticholinergic, so does not worsen confusion; moderate CYP2D6 inhibitor.', 0, ''),
    E('thiamine 100 mg daily: vitamin B1; prevents Wernicke encephalopathy/refeeding complications when feeding resumes after starvation.', 0, ''),
    E('zinc sulfate 220 mg (50 mg elemental) daily and ascorbic acid 500 mg BID: wound-healing nutrients (cell growth/immunity; collagen). Zinc with food; limit both to 10-14 days (order ends 11/03): prolonged zinc causes copper deficiency and anemia.', 0, ''),
    E('multivitamin with minerals daily (TWO duplicate active orders, both due 0900): nutrition support; ask pharmacy to discontinue one.', 0, ''),
    E('Ensure Enlive 1 bottle TID and Ensure Plus BID (duplicate supplements, 1,750 kcal/day): high-calorie/protein supplements between meals; record percent taken; refeeding caution; clarify one order.', 0, ''),
    E('ondansetron 4 mg IV q8h PRN nausea; zinc oxide 20% ointment PRN after each incontinence episode: 5-HT3 antagonist (QTc check, with donepezil and low K/Mg); skin protectant (apply to buttocks, perineum and folds, NOT into the wound).', 0, '')]

p3 = {'id': '3', 'title': 'Pharmacology and Medication Safety', 'step': 'Assessment / Analysis (and safe implementation)', 'minutes': 50,
      'intro': 'Use your drug guide and the MAR (click the medication name for the drug guide in the EHR). Part of your job is to question orders. Only active orders count; the LR bolus and LR maintenance orders are completed.',
      'items': [
          {'id': '3.1', 'type': 'table', 'points': 4.5, 'title': 'Medication List',
           'prompt': 'From the MAR and orders, choose the SIX medications that matter most to you before the 0900 medication pass. List brand and generic names.', 'where': 'MAR (click the medication name for the drug guide)',
           'cols': ['Medication (brand and generic)', 'Drug class and mechanism (10 words or fewer)', 'Why is THIS patient taking it?', 'Key nursing check / hold parameter'], 'widths': [1.55, 1.95, 1.7, 1.8], 'rows': 6, 'rowH': 0.68,
           'key': {'answer': 'Each row 0.75: medication is on this patient\'s MAR/orders (0.15), correct class and mechanism (0.2), correct indication for this patient (0.2), valid nursing check/hold parameter (0.2). Use the bank below.', 'elements': MEDBANK,
                   'rules': 'Maximum 4.5. Penalize a row that names a drug not on the chart (for example oxycodone, which the H&P mentions but is not ordered). Accept brand OR generic.', 'bank': True}},
          {'id': '3.2a', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'Vancomycin and Piperacillin-Tazobactam', 'where': 'MAR; Orders (vancomycin, trough/AUC order); Lab Results (creatinine, vancomycin level, cultures)',
           'prompt': 'Vancomycin 750 mg is due at 0900 and pip-tazo continues. Her creatinine is 0.87 (it was 1.35 on admission), the 10/21 level was 12.0, the nasal MRSA PCR was negative, and the wound culture shows gram-negative rods and gram-positive cocci in pairs. What will you check before and after the dose and when will you hold and call? What is wrong with how the 10/21 "trough" was timed? What question will you ask about continuing vancomycin?',
           'key': {'elements': [
               E('Check and hold: creatinine daily, urine output (hold and call for rise of 0.3 or more, output below 0.5 mL/kg/hr = 25.5 mL/hr for 51 kg, or level above 20); infuse over at least 90-120 minutes (rate no faster than 1 g/hr) to prevent infusion reaction; ask about ringing in the ears; vancomycin plus pip-tazo, lisinopril and furosemide increase kidney injury risk in an older adult; watch for diarrhea (C. difficile).', 0.5, ''),
               E('Timing: the order says draw 30 minutes BEFORE the dose; the level was collected at 1224, about 3.5 hours after the 0900 dose started, so it was near the peak, not a trough and cannot be used as one; tell pharmacy; today\'s level (if wanted) must be drawn before the 0900 dose.', 0.5, ''),
               E('Question: the MRSA nasal PCR was negative and the Gram stain shows organisms pip-tazo covers; the hospitalist plan is to stop vancomycin when the final culture shows no MRSA but the order auto-stops 10/23 0600 regardless: ask whether to continue or stop it today, and when the final culture will be back.', 0.5, '')]}},
          {'id': '3.2b', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 10, 'title': 'Pain Assessment and the Codeine Reaction', 'where': 'Orders (pain assessment/premedication, analgesics); Allergies; Nursing Admission Note',
           'prompt': 'She has dementia and cannot always report pain. How will you assess it (name the tool and what it scores, and her chart score)? Her orders are acetaminophen, tramadol 25 mg PRN for pain 4-6 and IV hydromorphone PRN for 7-10, and her pain is 3/10. Her codeine "allergy" is nausea and vomiting. What would you do before the next NPWT foam change (due in 48-72 hours) and how do you think about opioid choice?',
           'key': {'elements': [
               E('Tool: PAINAD (breathing, negative vocalization, facial expression, body language, consolability; each 0-2, total 0-10; 1-3 mild, 4-6 moderate, 7-10 severe); her admission PAINAD was 4 (grimaces with turning). Assess at rest AND with movement, before and after medication; use the 0-10 number when she can give it; behavior change (new agitation) may be pain or delirium.', 0.5, ''),
               E('Plan: acetaminophen on schedule (30-60 minutes before dressing changes and turning) but note 650 mg q6h = 2.6 g/day exceeds the 2 g limit the order lists for malnourished patients: ask; her baseline 3/10 is below the tramadol PRN range (4-6), so ask the provider for a specific premedication order before the foam change; low dose first (start low go slow), sedation and RR checks, bowel regimen, delirium watch.', 0.5, ''),
               E('Codeine: nausea/vomiting is an intolerance/side effect rather than an immune allergy, but clarify the reaction and document it; do not give codeine; tramadol is a related opioid that is activated like codeine (CYP2D6), also causes nausea and lowers the seizure threshold and is on the Beers list; a non-codeine opioid at the lowest dose with an antiemetic plan (ondansetron PRN: check QTc/potassium/magnesium with donepezil) and monitoring is reasonable; the provider and pharmacist choose.', 0.5, '')],
                   'rules': 'Do not require a specific drug change; credit sound reasoning. No credit for "give codeine because it is only nausea."'}},
          {'id': '3.2c', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2, 'lines': 14, 'title': 'The Obvious Action Is Wrong',
           'prompt': 'A busy colleague offers these shortcuts for Mrs. Whitcomb. Choose FOUR of the six. For each, write why it is unsafe for HER (use her data) and what you would do instead.\n(a) "She is septic and a bit dry: run a 1 L LR bolus and put her back on LR at 100 mL/hr."\n(b) "She only eats half, so give every supplement now and push 1,550 calories today."\n(c) "She gets agitated at night and pulls at the tubing: ask for lorazepam, or use mitts and a vest restraint."\n(d) "The right hip area is red: massage it to bring the circulation back."\n(e) "Put her on a donut cushion in the chair so the sacral wound touches nothing."\n(f) "Lay her fully on her side, 90 degrees, to get the sacrum free, and raise the head of the bed to 60 degrees after meals."',
           'key': {'answer': 'Any 4. Each 0.5: names why it is unsafe with a patient-specific reason (0.25) AND a safer action (0.25).', 'bank': True, 'elements': [
               E('(a) Fluids: EF about 35%, trace edema, net +549 mL in 24 h, lactate already 1.5, SBP 115, creatinine and sodium corrected; a large bolus or continuous fluid risks pulmonary edema. Safer: small boluses (250-500 mL) only if hypotensive or lactate rises, reassess lungs, SpO2, BP, weight and I&O after each; stop for crackles or dyspnea; the standard 30 mL/kg = 1,530 mL is not appropriate here.', 0.5, ''),
               E('(b) Refeeding: starvation then rapid calories can drop phosphorus (2.9 and falling), potassium and magnesium and cause arrhythmia, respiratory failure and fluid overload. Safer: follow the dietitian plan: start 10-20 kcal/kg (510-1,020 kcal), advance over 3-5 days, thiamine, daily electrolytes with replacement before advancing; ask which supplement order is intended (the two orders total 1,750 kcal).', 0.5, ''),
               E('(c) Benzodiazepines and restraints worsen delirium, falls and injury (Beers; the delirium order says avoid restraints, limit sedatives). Safer: find and treat the cause (pain by PAINAD, full bladder or soiled brief, hypoxia, infection, hunger), reorient, daughter at bedside, glasses/hearing aids, day-night routine, hide/secure NPWT tubing, frequent rounding or a sitter; call the provider if she is unsafe.', 0.5, ''),
               E('(d) Massage over a reddened bony area damages fragile capillaries and deep tissue. Safer: if it blanches it is only pressure-related erythema: offload with the 30-degree tilt, protective foam, keep skin dry, check with each turn; non-blanchable redness = stage 1, report and document.', 0.5, ''),
               E('(e) A donut ring concentrates pressure on the surrounding tissue and reduces blood flow (the support-surface order says no donut cushions). Safer: pressure-redistribution cushion, sit at most 1 hour (three times a day), lift/transfer by mechanical lift, return to the low-air-loss bed; confirm the chair order first (see 1.8).', 0.5, ''),
               E('(f) A 90-degree side-lying position loads the trochanter; raising the head of the bed beyond 30 degrees shears the sacrum. Safer: 30-degree tilt with wedges alternating left, back-tilt and right; HOB 30 degrees or less except at meals (sit upright for eating, then lower); limit time upright; use knee gatch and lift sheet; aspiration precautions per swallow evaluation.', 0.5, '')],
                   'rules': 'Maximum 2.0. Choose the best four; do not give extra credit for more than four.'}},
          {'id': '3.2d', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 9, 'title': 'Lisinopril, Carvedilol and Furosemide at 0900', 'where': 'MAR; Orders (hold parameters, potassium/creatinine monitoring); Flowsheets; Notes',
           'prompt': 'At 0900 lisinopril 20 mg, carvedilol 6.25 mg and furosemide 40 mg are due. Her BP is 115/71, HR 87, potassium 3.7, magnesium 1.7, creatinine 0.87, the hospitalist calls her "euvolemic to mildly dry," and she is on vancomycin and pip-tazo. What do you check, which drugs have hold parameters, what is missing, and what will you ask the provider before you give them?',
           'key': {'elements': [
               E('Check BP and HR (and rhythm) immediately before: hold and notify for SBP below 100 (lisinopril, carvedilol) or HR below 55 (carvedilol); give carvedilol with food; check potassium, creatinine, urine output, lung sounds and edema; do not stop the beta blocker abruptly in HFrEF.', 0.5, ''),
               E('Furosemide has NO hold parameter, and no weight is charted: ask for one (SBP, creatinine, potassium, volume status) and get today\'s bed-scale weight first; loop diuretic plus lisinopril plus two nephrotoxic antibiotics in a septic, "mildly dry" older adult risks acute kidney injury; furosemide also lowers potassium and magnesium (refeeding watch, QT risk with ondansetron/donepezil).', 0.5, ''),
               E('Context to report: Hgb 6.4 (critical, unaddressed); if she is transfused, ask how to manage volume (diuretic timing); donepezil plus carvedilol adds bradycardia; ask whether the ACE inhibitor and diuretic should be continued today while recovering from sepsis and prerenal injury; document the decision.', 0.5, '')]}},
          {'id': '3.3', 'type': 'boxes', 'points': 3, 'title': 'Math From the Chart', 'where': 'MAR, Orders, Nutrition notes, I&O, Lab Results',
           'prompt': 'Show your work in the box. Include units. Weight 51 kg.', 'subs': [
              {'label': 'a) Vancomycin 750 mg IV is supplied in 250 mL and ordered over 90 minutes. What pump rate in mL/hour? How many mg/kg is the 750 mg dose?', 'lines': 2},
              {'label': 'b) Acetaminophen is 650 mg every 6 hours. How many mg per 24 hours? The order\'s drug reference gives a maximum of 2 g/day for malnourished patients: by how much does her daily total exceed it, and what is the largest dose she could receive every 6 hours?', 'lines': 3},
              {'label': 'c) Ensure Enlive (350 kcal, 20 g protein) is ordered 3 times daily and Ensure Plus (350 kcal) twice daily. How many kcal per day do the supplements give? Her refeeding order says start at 10 to 20 kcal/kg/day: what is that range in kcal? Does the supplement total fit?', 'lines': 3},
              {'label': 'd) Her goal urine output is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hr. What is the minimum in mL per hour and in one 8-hour shift? Her last 24 hours intake was 2,409 mL and output 1,860 mL: what is the net balance?', 'lines': 3}],
           'key': {'elements': [
               E('a) 250 mL / 1.5 h = 166.7, about 167 mL/hr (0.4); 750 mg / 51 kg = 14.7 mg/kg (0.35)', 0.75, ''),
               E('b) 650 x 4 = 2,600 mg/24 h (0.25); 2,600 - 2,000 = 600 mg over (0.25); 2,000 / 4 = 500 mg per dose (0.25)', 0.75, ''),
               E('c) Enlive 3 x 350 = 1,050 + Plus 2 x 350 = 700 = 1,750 kcal/day (0.25); 10 x 51 = 510 to 20 x 51 = 1,020 kcal (0.25); 1,750 is more than the whole goal (about 1,550) and well above the refeeding start range: it does NOT fit (0.25)', 0.75, ''),
               E('d) 0.5 x 51 = 25.5 mL/hr (0.2); 25.5 x 8 = 204 mL per shift (0.2); 2,409 - 1,860 = +549 mL positive (0.35)', 0.75, '')],
                   'rules': 'Within each lettered problem award the amounts shown. Half credit for a correct method with an arithmetic or unit error. No credit without work shown. Accept 167 or 166.7 mL/hr and 14.7 or 15 mg/kg.'}},
      ]}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 4
p4 = {'id': '4', 'title': 'Nursing Diagnoses and Priorities', 'step': 'Diagnosis', 'minutes': 25,
      'intro': 'Use NANDA-I diagnoses. Write each as problem, "related to" (cause you can treat), and "as evidenced by" (specific data). Do not use a medical diagnosis as the "related to."',
      'items': [
          {'id': '4.1', 'type': 'table', 'points': 6, 'title': 'Prioritized Nursing Diagnoses', 'prompt': 'Write FOUR nursing diagnoses in priority order (1 = highest).',
           'cols': ['#', 'NANDA-I nursing diagnosis', 'Related to (etiology)', 'As evidenced by (at least 2 specific chart findings)'], 'widths': [0.4, 1.9, 2.0, 2.7], 'rows': 4, 'rowH': 1.0,
           'key': {'answer': 'Each row 1.5: valid, patient-appropriate diagnosis (0.5); "related to" is correct and is not a medical diagnosis (0.5); "as evidenced by" cites at least 2 specific data (0.5). Accept any defensible top diagnosis if the justification in 4.2 uses ABCs, Maslow or acuity.', 'bank': True,
                   'elements': [E('Impaired tissue integrity r/t prolonged pressure, shear, moisture and malnutrition AEB stage 4 sacral wound 6.9 x 5.6 x 3.8 cm with fascia exposed, undermining, Braden 12.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for infection (progression to sepsis or osteomyelitis) r/t infected wound over bone, diabetes (A1c 8.0), malnutrition AEB WBC 11.6, CRP 164, ESR 92, MRI coccyx edema equivocal, cultures positive for gram-negative rods and cocci.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for decreased cardiac output / Risk for ineffective peripheral tissue perfusion r/t critical anemia and HFrEF AEB Hgb 6.4 (7.0 on admission), EF about 35%, tachycardia history.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for imbalanced fluid volume (or Risk for excess fluid volume) r/t HFrEF, IV fluids/transfusion and diuretic during sepsis AEB net +549 mL/24 h, trace edema, no weights charted.', 0, ''),
                                E('Imbalanced nutrition: less than body requirements (accept equivalent wording) r/t inadequate intake and increased needs for healing AEB intake 50-75% of meals, albumin 2.3, prealbumin 9, temporal wasting.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for electrolyte imbalance (refeeding) r/t increased calorie intake after prolonged poor intake AEB phosphorus 3.4 -> 2.9, potassium 3.7, magnesium 1.7.', 0, ''),
                                E('Acute pain r/t tissue injury, debridement and repositioning/dressing changes AEB PAINAD 4 with grimacing on turning, pain 3-5/10.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for acute confusion r/t dementia, infection, age, unfamiliar environment and opioids AEB oriented to person only on admission, drowsy, baseline mild confusion.', 0, ''),
                                E('Impaired skin integrity (incontinence-associated dermatitis) / Urge urinary incontinence r/t overactive bladder, cognitive impairment, loose stools AEB moist red buttocks and perineum, incontinent urine and stool.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for unstable blood glucose level r/t diabetes, infection, variable intake and basal insulin AEB glucose 176-184, A1c 8.0, eating 50-75%.', 0, ''),
                                E('Impaired bed mobility / Impaired transfer ability r/t frailty, contractures, dementia AEB total assist with mechanical lift, mild knee contractures.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for falls; Compromised family coping (daughter); Risk for compromised dignity (accept with data).', 0, '')],
                   'rules': 'Maximum 6.0. A problem-only statement with no etiology or evidence earns 0.5 for that row at most. "Stage 4 pressure injury" is a medical diagnosis and cannot be the "related to."'}},
          {'id': '4.2', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2, 'lines': 7, 'title': 'Why This Order?', 'prompt': 'Explain why your #1 diagnosis is first (use ABCs, Maslow, or acuity). Name one lower-ranked diagnosis that could become the top priority and what finding would move it up.',
           'key': {'elements': [E('Applies a framework: airway/breathing/circulation, Maslow physiologic needs, or acuity (what could harm her soonest).', 0.5, ''), E('Ties ranking to patient data (Hgb 6.4, sepsis markers, SBP/HR, wound, intake).', 0.5, ''), E('Names a valid lower-ranked risk (infection/sepsis, fluid overload, refeeding, delirium, bleeding).', 0.5, ''), E('Gives a concrete trigger (temperature above 101 F, HR above 110, SBP below 100, new confusion, urine output below 25.5 mL/hr; phosphorus below 2.5; crackles or SpO2 below 92%; bright red blood in the canister).', 0.5, '')]}},
      ]}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 5
SMART = 'SMART goal = Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound, written about the PATIENT (not the nurse).'
p5 = {'id': '5', 'title': 'Planning and Implementation', 'step': 'Planning / Implementation', 'minutes': 50,
      'intro': 'Plan the care for the 0700-1500 shift. ' + SMART,
      'items': [
          {'id': '5.1', 'type': 'table', 'points': 4, 'title': 'Plan for Your Priority Diagnosis #1: ________________', 'cols': ['SMART goal for this shift', 'Nursing interventions (at least 5: what, how often)', 'Rationale', 'I / C / D'], 'widths': [1.6, 3.1, 1.7, 0.6], 'rows': 4, 'rowH': 1.0,
           'prompt': 'Use the top diagnosis from 4.1. I = independent, C = collaborative, D = delegated.',
           'key': {'answer': 'Goal 1.0 (measurable number AND time frame AND patient-centered); interventions 2.0 (at least 5 safe, specific, with frequency: about 0.4 each); rationale 1.0 (correct for at least 4 interventions). Accept any diagnosis chosen in 4.1. Model answers:', 'bank': True,
                   'elements': [E('Impaired tissue integrity: Goal: through 1500 the NPWT stays sealed at -125 mmHg continuous with no off-time over 2 hours, all 4 scheduled turns (0800, 1000, 1200, 1400) are documented on a 30-degree tilt, and no new non-blanchable area appears on trochanters, ischium, heels or coccyx. Interventions: turn q2h on 30-degree tilt (left, back-tilt, right) with wedges and a lift sheet; HOB 30 degrees or less except meals; check NPWT seal, canister, tubing every shift and after every turn and every incontinence episode; clean urine/stool right away with pH-balanced no-rinse cleanser, barrier ointment on buttocks/perineum (NOT in the wound), one breathable underpad, tubing off the skin; float heels, inspect bony prominences with each turn; give acetaminophen 30-60 min before turns/foam changes and use PAINAD; chair only after the order is clarified, mechanical lift, cushion (no donut), 1 hour maximum, then back to the low-air-loss bed; Braden each shift. Rationale: pressure relief and shear reduction remove the cause; an airtight seal is needed for NPWT to work; moisture and stool cause infection and skin damage.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for infection/sepsis: Goal: temperature stays below 100.4 F, HR below 100, SBP 100 or higher, no new confusion, and urine output at least 25.5 mL/hr (204 mL per shift) through 1500. Interventions: vital signs and sepsis screen q4h and with any change; mark periwound borders and describe drainage/odor; hand hygiene and aseptic NPWT care; vancomycin and pip-tazo on time (extended 4-h pip-tazo infusion), question the vancomycin stop; follow cultures, creatinine, WBC; report CRP/ESR repeat need; watch for diarrhea (C. difficile). Rationale: early detection prevents septic shock; timely antibiotics maintain levels.', 0, ''),
                                E('Imbalanced nutrition / refeeding risk: Goal: she eats at least 75% of two meals and takes 2 supplements with percent recorded by 1500, and phosphorus, potassium and magnesium stay at or above 2.5, 3.5 and 1.5. Interventions: set-up and feeding assistance, sit upright for meals then lower HOB, offer protein first; record percent eaten and supplement volume; give thiamine and ONE multivitamin; clarify the duplicate supplement orders and calorie goal with the dietitian; get today\'s weight; review electrolytes and report phosphorus below 2.5; check swallow clearance; glucose before meals. Rationale: protein and calories are needed for healing; slow advancement prevents refeeding syndrome.', 0, ''),
                                E('Risk for decreased cardiac output/excess fluid volume (Hgb 6.4 with HFrEF): Goal: provider notified of Hgb 6.4 before 0900 and an order plan is documented; HR below 100, SBP 100 or higher, SpO2 94% or higher, lungs clear through 1500. Interventions: SBAR early; assess symptoms and bleeding; weight, I&O, lung sounds q4h; hold/clarify per orders; if transfused, slow rate and TACO watch. Rationale: the failing heart cannot compensate for anemia or volume.', 0, ''),
                                E('Acute pain: Goal: PAINAD below 4 at rest and below 5 with repositioning, RR 12 or more and awake, through 1500. Interventions: PAINAD with each vital set and turn; premedicate 30-60 min before care; non-drug measures; sedation/RR before opioids; ask for a premedication order. Rationale: unrelieved pain causes agitation, delirium and avoids turning.', 0, '')],
                   'rules': 'A goal without a number or time frame earns 0.5 (of 1.0). Interventions that are not specific ("monitor patient") earn no credit.'}},
          {'id': '5.2', 'type': 'table', 'points': 3, 'title': 'Time-Sequenced Plan for the Shift', 'where': 'MAR, Orders, Notes, Sticky notes',
           'prompt': 'For each hour, list your priority actions IN ORDER. Include medications (and which you will question), wound/NPWT care, repositioning, nutrition, assessments, communication, and say why that time.',
           'cols': ['Time', 'Priority actions in order', 'Why at this time'], 'widths': [0.7, 4.3, 2.0], 'rows': [['0700'], ['0800'], ['0900'], ['1000'], ['1100'], ['1200'], ['1300'], ['1400']], 'rowH': 0.72,
           'key': {'answer': 'Score 0.375 per hour row with an appropriate, sequenced, patient-specific plan.', 'bank': True, 'elements': [
               E('0700: bedside report with safety checks (ID and allergy bands, bed alarm, low bed, call light); vital signs and sepsis screen; look at the 0500 labs (Hgb 6.4 critical, phosphorus 2.9); NPWT seal, canister and tubing; skin check and turn on a 30-degree tilt (due 0800); PAINAD; bed-scale weight before breakfast; review orders and list clarifications (Hgb, duplicate multivitamin and supplements, chair order, furosemide parameter, vancomycin level/stop).', 0, ''),
               E('0730-0800: glucose before breakfast and lispro per scale (0730); meal set-up and feeding assistance, record percent eaten; toileting/perineal care with barrier ointment; call the hospitalist (SBAR) about Hgb 6.4 and other clarifications BEFORE the 0900 medications; 0800 turn.', 0, ''),
               E('0900: medication pass after clarification: check BP/HR; vancomycin over 90-120 min (or hold if told); lisinopril/carvedilol/furosemide per parameters and answers; thiamine, mirabegron, vitamin C, zinc with food, ONE multivitamin; Ensure Enlive; lung sounds and urine output.', 0, ''),
               E('1000: Ensure Plus; 1000 turn; PT may mobilize with mechanical lift if the chair order is clarified (no donut, cushion, 1 hour maximum); premedicate before moving; if a transfusion is ordered, consent from the daughter, double check, baseline vital signs and lung sounds.', 0, ''),
               E('1100: reassess pain (PAINAD) and delirium; mouth care, hydration, bowel check; NPWT seal check after each turn; document percent intake and calorie count; update daughter (health care proxy) about plan and answer questions.', 0, ''),
               E('1130-1200: glucose and lispro before lunch; acetaminophen 1200 (check total for the day); upright for lunch with feeding assistance then HOB back to 30 degrees; 1200 turn on tilt; incontinence care and skin check of ischium/sacrum if up in the chair.', 0, ''),
               E('1300: vital signs; reassess I&O and weight trend; wound/NPWT and heels check; social work/case management and dietitian updates; teach daughter or facility staff (turning, NPWT alarms); document.', 0, ''),
               E('1400: pip-tazo (extended infusion); Ensure Enlive; 1400 turn; final skin and NPWT check; handoff with open issues (Hgb plan, vancomycin decision, phosphorus/magnesium/potassium, chair tolerance, daughter\'s questions, neglect-screening status).', 0, '')],
                   'rules': 'Give no credit for a row that lists only "meds" or "assessment." Penalize a plan that gives every 0900 medication without addressing the critical hemoglobin and the order questions. Turns should be about every 2 hours (0800, 1000, 1200, 1400).'}},
          {'id': '5.3', 'type': 'boxes', 'points': 2, 'title': 'Calling the Provider (SBAR)', 'where': 'Lab Results; MAR; Orders; Notes',
           'prompt': 'At 0745 you review the 0500 labs and the 0900 medications. Hemoglobin is 6.4 (6.5 yesterday, 7.0 on admission), there is no transfusion order or anemia work-up, enoxaparin was given at 0600, and she has HFrEF. HR 87, BP 115/71, no bleeding seen, 40-60 mL serosanguineous in the NPWT canister over 12 hours. You call Dr. Patel (hospitalist). Write what you would say.',
           'subs': [{'label': 'S: Situation', 'lines': 3}, {'label': 'B: Background', 'lines': 3}, {'label': 'A: Assessment', 'lines': 3}, {'label': 'R: Recommendation / request', 'lines': 3}],
           'key': {'elements': [E('S: identifies self, patient, room and the specific problem: critical hemoglobin 6.4 (a downward trend) with no plan, current vital signs HR 87, BP 115/71.', 0.5, ''), E('B: 84-year-old, stage 4 sacral wound after debridement 10/21 and NPWT, early sepsis improving, HFrEF EF about 35%, hemoglobin 7.0 -> 6.5 -> 6.4, enoxaparin 40 mg given 0600, full code, daughter is health care proxy, last labs and the volume she has received.', 0.5, ''), E('A: states a concern (critical anemia that her heart may not tolerate; possible bleeding/ongoing loss or dilution; not sure of cause); no symptoms or bleeding seen now.', 0.5, ''), E('R: asks whether to type and screen and transfuse (threshold), anemia work-up, hold enoxaparin, repeat CBC and fewer blood draws, parameters for furosemide today; mentions the other clarifications briefly (duplicate multivitamin/supplements, vancomycin level/stop, chair order); read-back of orders and agreed time to reassess.', 0.5, '')]}},
          {'id': '5.4', 'type': 'matrix', 'points': 2, 'title': 'Delegation', 'prompt': 'Mark the LOWEST level of staff who may be assigned each task for this patient.', 'cols': ['RN only', 'LPN/LVN', 'UAP/PCT'],
           'rows': ['Turn her to a 30-degree tilt with a lift sheet and wedges, float her heels, and keep the wound vac tubing off her skin', 'Compare her 0900 vital signs and urine output with her sepsis call parameters and decide whether to notify the provider', 'Give the 0900 oral carvedilol and lisinopril after the RN has checked the BP and heart rate and clarified the hold parameters', 'Perform the NPWT foam change and measure the wound'],
           'key': {'answer': ['UAP/PCT', 'RN only', 'LPN/LVN', 'RN only'], 'rules': '0.5 per row. Accept RN-only for row 3 if the student explains the RN retains the assessment and clarification responsibility. Row 4: per the order, foam changes are done by the WOC nurse or a trained RN.'}},
          {'id': '5.5', 'type': 'box', 'points': 3, 'lines': 18, 'title': 'Communication, Dignity, Reporting and Discharge Back to Long-Term Care', 'where': 'Social history; Orders (social work/case management; delirium); Case Management and PT notes',
           'prompt': 'Her daughter, the health care proxy, arrives upset. Write (1) what you will tell her about the wound vac and the plan and how you will include her, (2) three ways you will protect your patient\'s dignity, (3) what your responsibility is because a stage 4 pressure injury developed in the long-term care facility and an order asks for neglect screening, and (4) what the receiving long-term care facility must be told and have ready before she returns.',
           'key': {'elements': [
               E('Daughter/proxy: introduce yourself; explain in plain language what NPWT does, that the foam is changed every 48-72 hours with pain medicine, the alarms and what to do if it stops; why she is turned every 2 hours; teach-back; ask about baseline cognition, preferences, pain behaviors and prior wound care; invite her to participate; she consents for the patient (capacity limited) so confirm proxy documents; give a contact for questions; ask her not to adjust the vac.', 0.75, ''),
               E('Dignity (any 3): address her by name; explain every step even if she may not understand; privacy and covering during dressing changes and incontinence care (door/curtain); do not speak about her as if absent; prompt, gentle incontinence care; maintain clothing, hair and glasses/hearing aids; offer choices and respect her routine; avoid restraints and medication to quiet her.', 0.5, ''),
               E('Reporting: nurses are mandated reporters of suspected abuse or neglect of a dependent adult (laws vary by state); a deep facility-acquired pressure injury may need to be reported through the proper channel (adult protective services / state long-term care ombudsman / health department per hospital policy). Your role: document objective facts (location, size, stage, timeline from the daughter, photos only as policy allows) without accusing; tell the charge nurse and social work/case management (neglect screening was ordered 10/20 1400 with no documented result); follow the chain of command and policy; do not investigate yourself; document present-on-admission status; protect the daughter and patient from retaliation.', 1.0, ''),
               E('Discharge handoff (any 5): nursing/physician report to the facility (SBAR); NPWT settings (-125 mmHg continuous), foam-change schedule and WOC/home health plan, supplies and vendor confirmed BEFORE transfer; low-air-loss mattress, chair cushion (no donut), mechanical lift; 30-degree turning q2h; HOB 30 degrees or less; nutrition plan, supplements, weights, electrolytes; antibiotic stop date or ID/bone plan and lab follow-up (ESR/CRP); glucose and insulin plan; hemoglobin follow-up; baseline cognition and delirium plan; follow-up with surgery/wound clinic; goals-of-care conversation; teach facility staff; PT/OT recommendations.', 0.75, '')]}},
      ]}

